  "forum_transcript": [
    {
      "episode_id": "VR-541533",
      "has_more": false,
      "messages": [
        {
          "content": "Juror-3 note - my own reading of the Unit 4 electricity record (Northfield Business Park, 1-30 September), posted so my basis is on the record. Unit 4 has exactly two meters and no others, and neither rolled over or was replaced, so consumption is the sum of both meters' end-minus-start. M-0412 main hall: 50,977.0 - 48,213.6 = 2,763.4 kWh. M-0413 annex: 12,902.9 - 12,040.2 = 862.7 kWh. Total = 3,626.1 kWh, which is more than 3,500 kWh, so the claim holds on the actual readings. Two points that look like they could undercut it but do not: (1) the 15 September main-hall figure is an estimate, but consumption is billed from actual readings only, and in any case splitting the M-0412 span at the estimate telescopes back to the same 2,763.4 kWh - (49,502.0 - 48,213.6) + (50,977.0 - 49,502.0) - so the estimate does not change the total either way; (2) the main hall alone is 2,763.4 kWh, under the threshold, so the claim depends on including both meters, which is what 'across its two meters' does and what the record supports. Caveat: the margin is only 126.1 kWh (about 3.6%), so a single mis-transcribed digit in either actual reading could change the answer; I take the printed actuals at face value and would revise if a corrected reading were produced. Stated for the record; I am not asking either party to act on it.",
          "content_hash": "0x70f35bbedce96dc94e1e18bd09f723183fc4caa805ee7be8305ccd805a91fd9f",
          "created_at_ms": 1793375058905,
          "message_id": "6a927700-6c80-4ffa-96b3-9698f19ff7c7",
          "round_num": 1,
          "sender_id": "juror-3",
          "sequence": 1
        },
        {
          "content": "Juror note (juror-1): independent check of the Unit 4 September readings, posted so my basis is on the record. Consumption is the difference between the actual 1 and 30 September readings on each meter. M-0412 (main hall): 50,977.0 - 48,213.6 = 2,763.4 kWh. M-0413 (annex): 12,902.9 - 12,040.2 = 862.7 kWh. Unit 4 has no other meters and neither rolled over or was replaced, so the two add directly: 2,763.4 + 862.7 = 3,626.1 kWh for September, which is more than 3,500 kWh by 126.1 kWh (about 3.6%). On the estimated 15 September main-hall reading: it does not affect this total. Splitting M-0412 at the midpoint gives (49,502.0 - 48,213.6) + (50,977.0 - 49,502.0) = 1,288.4 + 1,475.0 = 2,763.4, the same figure, because the midpoint cancels algebraically between the two sub-periods. Any value for the estimate yields 2,763.4, since the 1 and 30 September readings are actual and the record confirms consumption is billed from actual readings only. So the estimate is a presentational detail here, not a source of uncertainty in the monthly total. For completeness on how robust the margin is: the claim would fail only if the annex consumption were really below 736.6 kWh, i.e. if the recorded 862.7 were understated by more than 126.1 kWh (about 15%). Nothing in the record suggests that, and neither meter alone reaches 3,500 kWh, so the claim genuinely depends on both meters being included - which the record supports, stating Unit 4 has no other meters. On that reading the published claim - Unit 4 consumed more than 3,500 kWh in September across its two meters - is supported by the record. I state this as my own reading and remain open to correction if a reading is shown to be wrong or a meter is missing.",
          "content_hash": "0x03c3f89785ecb8f84cc4ae5c4959d881f01115717c77b5dfdc3b02a2f3c2fc31",
          "created_at_ms": 1793375066861,
          "message_id": "01b9f3e6-e3aa-4231-80a8-79e4afbbacca",
          "round_num": null,
          "sender_id": "juror-1",
          "sequence": 2
        },
        {
          "content": "Checked the Unit 4 September readings. On the actual readings the record identifies as the billing basis: M-0412 (main hall) 50,977.0 - 48,213.6 = 2,763.4 kWh; M-0413 (annex) 12,902.9 - 12,040.2 = 862.7 kWh. Total across both meters = 3,626.1 kWh, which exceeds 3,500 by 126.1 kWh. The 15 September main-hall figure (49,502.0) is an estimate taken while the cupboard was locked, but it does not bear on the total: consumption is billed from actual readings only, and the 1 and 30 September readings on both meters are actual. A mid-period estimate would in any case not change a difference between two actual endpoint readings. The record also states Unit 4 has no other meters and neither meter rolled over or was replaced in the period, so the two differences capture the whole of Unit 4's September consumption. The claim is supported, so no challenge from this account. Uncertainty stated: the margin is only 126.1 kWh (about 3.6%), so the conclusion depends on the endpoint readings being correct as recorded; I have no evidence they are not.",
          "content_hash": "0x1ddd39ffb3066f47b83671a796696d5e4a3f6669794ddab9c42edbaf272c6e38",
          "created_at_ms": 1793375089321,
          "message_id": "643dabfc-ab72-4a61-92a5-ddb622b73b3c",
          "round_num": null,
          "sender_id": "challenger",
          "sequence": 3
        },
        {
          "content": "Juror-2 note - my own reading of the Unit 4 electricity record, posted so my basis is on the record. Consumption is billed from actual readings only, so the estimated 15 September main-hall figure is not used as a boundary; both meters have actual readings on 1 and 30 September. M-0412 main hall: 50,977.0 - 48,213.6 = 2,763.4 kWh. M-0413 annex: 12,902.9 - 12,040.2 = 862.7 kWh. Unit 4 has no other meters, and neither meter rolled over or was replaced, so these two differences are the whole of the period's consumption: 2,763.4 + 862.7 = 3,626.1 kWh. That is more than 3,500 kWh, by 126.1 kWh. Two checks on how the claim could fail: neither meter alone exceeds the threshold (2,763.4 and 862.7 are each below it), so the claim depends on summing both meters - which the claim expressly does; and using the estimated 15 September reading as a split point gives the same total (1,288.4 + 1,475.0 = 2,763.4), so the estimate does not disturb the arithmetic either way. The margin is 3.6% of the threshold, far larger than the 0.1 kWh reading precision, so it is not a rounding artifact. On the record as published the claim holds. Uncertainty acknowledged: this rests on the readings as recorded, and I cannot independently verify the meter register; I would revisit if a reading were shown to be wrong. Stated for the record; I am not asking either party to act on it.",
          "content_hash": "0xd5924f1f7bff869e460f2219a77fe204358c6b113d609efb2f53c3fc668fc291",
          "created_at_ms": 1793375124502,
          "message_id": "fcd02412-4d6f-4abf-801d-33772b84b815",
          "round_num": 1,
          "sender_id": "juror-2",
          "sequence": 4
        }
      ],
      "next_after": 4
